1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,660
 Welcome to Long-Term Agentic Memory with LangGraph, built-in partnership with LangChain.

2
00:00:06,660 --> 00:00:10,280
 I'm delighted to welcome back Harrison Chase, co-founder and CEO of LangChain.

3
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:11,100
 Thanks, Andrew.

4
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:14,480
 Recently, we've seen many agentic applications being built,

5
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:18,600
 and this has helped us to develop a mental framework that is useful to think about

6
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:20,740
 for when you're adding memory to agents.

7
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:22,880
 We would like to share that with learners.

8
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:26,300
 More and more AI applications persist over time,

9
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:29,200
 and this really drives the need for agent memory.

10
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:32,320
 An example would be an AI personal assistant.

11
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:35,080
 Exactly. Assistants are a great example.

12
00:00:35,080 --> 00:00:38,060
 The more they learn, the better they are at future tasks.

13
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:44,260
 To add memory to an agent, you must first figure out what information to store in long-term memory,

14
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:48,760
 and also, when it comes time to use the information, what to retrieve.

15
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:50,540
 First, on what to store.

16
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:57,680
 Chatbots initially just store conversational history in the context memory at each turn of a conversation.

17
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:01,980
 But agents that act for you over time need long-term memory.

18
00:01:01,980 --> 00:01:08,480
 For example, a calendar agent might need to persist information about meetings over long periods

19
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:11,700
 and across multiple invocations of the agent.

20
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:14,300
 Then comes retrieval.

21
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:18,540
 Retrieval will take information from memory and insert it into the alarm context.

22
00:01:18,540 --> 00:01:22,640
 Harrison will show you how to figure out when and what to retrieve.

23
00:01:22,860 --> 00:01:27,520
 And additionally, we also need to decide when to update the stored information.

24
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:38,560
 Right. To address these questions, we've found that it's useful to think about three types of memories.

25
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:39,720
 Semantic memories.

26
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:43,320
 These are facts like important birthdays for a calendar agent.

27
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:45,440
 Next is episodic memories.

28
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:49,260
 These are experiences that can help an agent remember how to do tasks.

29
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:53,740
 And finally, there's procedural memories, which are rules for an agent to follow.

30
00:01:53,740 --> 00:02:09,000
 To help manage memory, we've created a new library, LangMem, that supports a vector database that provides searchable, shareable, persistent storage that can be updated immediately by the agent or in the background by a helper agent.

31
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:15,520
 In this course, you'll build a useful email assistant that demonstrates all of these concepts using LangMem.

32
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:18,420
 Several people have worked to create this course.

33
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:27,240
 I'd like to thank from Langchain, Lance Martin, Wolf Fuhin Thorn, and Nuno Campos, and from DeepLand.ai, Jeff Lardwik.

34
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:30,220
 All right, let's get started on the first lesson.

